    <Group elementId="CPLX-7062" textLabel="long-chain acyl-CoA oxidase" type="Complex">
      <Xref identifier="CPLX-7062" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="450" centerY="415" width="140" height="100" textColor="666666" fontName="Arial" fontSize="10" borderColor="9900CC" borderStyle="Dashed" borderWidth="2.0" fillColor="F5E6FF" shapeType="Rectangle" zOrder="-1" />
      <Comment>|FRAME: TAX-3702| has six acyl-CoA oxidase (ACX) genes. &lt;i&gt;In vitro&lt;/i&gt; enzyme assays have determined substrate specificities for |FRAME: AT4G16760| (long chain), |FRAME: AT5G65110| (very-long chain), |FRAME: AT1G06290| (medium chain) and |FRAME: AT3G51840| (short chain). &lt;i&gt;In vivo&lt;/i&gt; studies of enzyme activities using each ACX mutants confirmed &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; results |CITS: [15743450]|. &lt;i&gt;In vivo&lt;/i&gt;, ACX1 is primarily responsible for C16-C20 substrates, ACX3 for C12 and C14, and ACX4 for C4 and C6. Expressing ACX1 in |FRAME: TAX-562| followed by enzyme assay indicated that ACX1 is active with both medium- and long-chain acyl-CoAs. The activity with mono-unsaturated acyl-CoAs is slightly higher than with saturated. ACX1 showed maximum activity with C14-CoA. &lt;i&gt;In vivo&lt;/i&gt; study of ACX1 mutant indicated ACX1 is primarily responsible for C16-C20 substrates. Genetic evidence indicated a role of ACX1 and ACX5 in |FRAME: PWY-735|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="CPLX-7062" />
